WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.197 --> 00:00:02.780
(lively music)

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.403
- Davos was the scene.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.113
Everybody was there.

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:18.392
You have CEOs

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:19.950
from some of the biggest
companies in the world,

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.950
heads of state, the president was there.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.750
I don't remember anyone
taking coronavirus seriously.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.100
Everyone's always enthusiastic at Davos.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.540
No one makes friends there
by predicting doom and gloom.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.490
- This seems to be the
decade of shared success.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.620
- [All] Together we can.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.653
Together, we will.

00:00:37.653 --> 00:00:41.970
- I was hesitant to say anything
because being a scientist,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.770
driven by facts and data, the
absence of facts and data,

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:46.910
I didn't want to make any conjecture.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.830
So I remember in retrospect,

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.740
I probably made statements
that were perhaps too neutral.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.370
I think all the right
things are happening.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.900
I mean, you've seen some of the actions

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.180
the Chinese government has taken.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.270
I know critical disease control
agencies around the world

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.400
are getting really active.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.284
So we all have to wait and see
there's a lot we don't know.

00:01:02.284 --> 00:01:06.030
I don't think I was pressing it in saying

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.730
the economic trajectory of the US economy

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.670
would continue in 2020.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.380
People are relatively
confident that global growth

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.750
will sustain through 2020.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.477
The chance of an economic slowdown

00:01:15.477 --> 00:01:18.730
barring some sort of an exogenous event.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.030
We had an exogenous event.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:21.750
I guess I was right about that.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.300
- I remember watching CEO of
Goldman Sachs on the DJ booth

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.260
at a Salesforce party, just like 10 deep,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.550
shoulder to shoulder.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.403
It all feels like a totally
different planet now.

00:01:34.763 --> 00:01:37.140
These are people who should have had

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.820
the best sight lines in the
world into what was going on.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.660
These companies that
have operations in China,

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.000
where this virus had been
circulating for weeks.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.830
And in retrospect, it
probably should have informed

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.950
how we approach things
here 'cause we were, what,

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.443
about two months behind them.

00:01:53.949 --> 00:01:56.960
There was a sense that the
economy was just too strong

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.463
and America was just too resilient.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.870
Even as some parts of the economy

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:03.340
are showing positive signs,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.780
what happened during the month of March

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.080
will fundamentally
reshape American business

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.480
for the next decade.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.780
March started on a very high note

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.100
and it ended with the economy
just incomplete taters.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.170
And that 31 days where
just everything changed.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:27.186
(orchestral music)

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:38.970
- [Woman] The coronavirus-

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.190
- [Man] Is fanning out
across Asia and Europe.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:44.880
- [Woman] There may be additional
cases that we identified.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.540
I do want to be imperative of that.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.330
- [Man] Stocks, they
fell hard again today.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.473
Economic fears, many
unknowns rattling investors.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:56.700
- [Man] All non-essential businesses

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.093
shall close to their physical workplaces,

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.910
- [Woman] Police were
called after some customers

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.233
started fighting over
toilet paper and water.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.530
- [Man] This is our new reality right now.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.173
It is absolutely staggering
where we find ourselves.

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.641
(indistinct)

00:03:24.641 --> 00:03:25.474
- How'd you get that?

00:03:25.474 --> 00:03:26.680
- Happy to do it.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.513
Happy to do it.

00:03:27.513 --> 00:03:28.377
I want to be able to get back

00:03:28.377 --> 00:03:30.313
and look at it for posterity's sake.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.920
We're really good at opening hotels

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.760
but if you were to ask me
in the 36 or seven years

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:41.760
I've been doing this,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.010
how many hotels have I
actually presided over closing,

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.810
it would be a very, very short list.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.240
- I'm currently sitting actually

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.357
in the Baccarat lounge at Bellagio.

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:55.150
This building is vacant.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.363
It's a bit surreal.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.420
It's very different.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.740
- This is our trading desk.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.690
So for our agricultural commodities

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.610
and you can see that it is empty.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.230
Typically it's very busy.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:09.403
It's very noisy.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.740
- We designed this headquarters
campus in which I'm sitting

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.960
for about 5,000 people.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.633
Right now, there are roughly
300 people on a good day.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.847
- I started putting out a
Friday note to employees

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:26.750
on Friday the 13th,

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.590
which was most people's
last day in the office.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:32.840
It said Friday, March 13th,

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.110
I will stop comparing
this week to last week.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:36.850
We are in unchartered territory.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.000
First, take care of
yourselves and your families

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.390
as we run our business.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.260
Follow company and local guidance.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.349
Use judgment and humanity
with individual situations.

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:47.540
Show leadership with
people who are worried.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:49.653
And remember, we have a company to run.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.740
- I'm Chris Nassetta, CEO of Hilton.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:00.120
- I'm the senior vice president
of corporate affairs at 3M.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.640
- I'm the head of manufacturing

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.570
for Toyota Motor North America.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.700
- I am the CEO of Novartis.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.630
- I'm JetBlue's president
and chief operating officer.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.610
- CEO and chairman of Proctor and Gamble.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.550
- Chief executive officer
of IMAX Corporation.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.590
- I am chairman and CEO of Cargill.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.600
We're in the privileged
position of making food.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.570
- Chairman and CEO of Goldman Sachs.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:24.010
- Acting CEO and president
of MGM Resorts International.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.910
- CEO of US Wealth
Management for JP Morgan.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.940
- Deputy commissioner and
chief operating officer

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.400
of the NBA.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.406
- So coming into 2020,

00:05:31.406 --> 00:05:36.406
the US is in year 11 of the
longest economic expansion

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:37.780
since World War II.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.170
Millions of jobs have
been added to the economy.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.750
The stock market keeps
hitting record heights.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.300
It looked like 2020 was
gonna be another good year.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.047
- [Crowd] Three, two, one.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:55.640
- New years going into 2020
felt like any other new year's.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.080
We were seven months into our fiscal year

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.490
and things were going well.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:03.290
- I was two weeks into my
job as a first time CEO.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:06.490
- We had record Box Office
of over a billion dollars.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:07.360
- You know, when we look back,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.143
we see an auto market that
was pretty high actually.

00:06:10.143 --> 00:06:14.370
- Our sales for plus 6%,
our earnings were plus 18%.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:15.240
- We were looking forward

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.600
to bringing on a new aircraft type.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:17.847
Now the Airbus 220.

00:06:17.847 --> 00:06:19.610
- How we would evolve

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:22.200
the presentation of our game digitally.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.710
- We were all sort of having
a similar conversation

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.290
about like, gosh times are good,

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.530
but there's just sort
of this haunting feeling

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:31.183
like something's out there.

00:06:36.676 --> 00:06:40.780
- The pandemic showed that
strong economy that it was thin,

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:45.290
it was a veneer of shrink in
that companies were profitable,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.900
making a lot of money
and unemployment was low.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.890
But it really exposed these weaknesses

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.840
where wages had not really moved.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.530
People are not by and large
wealthier than they were

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:56.363
10 years ago.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.193
- The jobs that we saw grow the most

00:07:00.193 --> 00:07:02.833
tended to be of the alternative variety.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:05.980
There's contingent work, part-time work,

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:07.630
independent work, gig work.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:11.820
And that also brought with it
a certain moment of fragility.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.350
- Companies have spent
trillions of dollars

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.500
over the last decade
buying back their own stock

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:17.593
and paying dividends.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:20.810
- Profits were growing.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:22.750
But you might've worried
about, for example,

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:24.350
the rate of which companies

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:25.950
are not reinvesting into their businesses

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:26.920
or into the economy.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.670
The classic case of a lot
of short-term orientation

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:33.166
without kind of big
investments into the future.

00:07:33.166 --> 00:07:37.900
- The modern economy,
it's powered by technology

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.668
and it's this just in time,
it's perfectly efficient.

00:07:40.668 --> 00:07:42.690
And in some ways it is,

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:43.790
and in some ways that's been really good

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:45.900
for consumers in particular,

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.430
but the flip side of
efficiency is resiliency.

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:49.640
If there's no fat baked in,

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.770
there's no cushion for
when things get bad.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.430
- [Reporter] A mysterious
respiratory illness

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:56.340
has healthcare workers on alert.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:59.130
At least 45 people have
contacted the virus

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.930
believed to be connected to animals

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:02.653
at a market in central China.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:07.460
P&amp;G has people all over China.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.690
We don't have plants in Wu Han,

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.470
we have a distribution center.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:13.080
So as soon as this happened,

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.910
our first concern was making sure

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:16.270
we took care of our employees.

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:18.940
- We knew there was some sort of

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:22.170
emerging viral issue in December.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.780
Our health and safety
team had flagged in Wu Han

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:29.040
because we have five hotels in
Wu Han, something's going on.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.530
- It felt like a just China issue for us

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:35.040
because a lot of what we make
is for domestic consumption.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.640
And so certainly the
impacts that we looked at,

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:41.520
implications that we looked at
were largely limited to China

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.723
and perhaps parts of
Southeast Asia at that time.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:47.250
- I don't think anybody thought

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.650
even when this was
impacting Macau in China,

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.945
that it would distance
itself, this close to us here

00:08:51.945 --> 00:08:54.603
and end up in our ultimate shutdown.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:57.830
- So there's this disconnect

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:00.727
between the sort of
bad rumblings out there

00:09:00.727 --> 00:09:04.233
and the bad headlines coming
out of China and across Europe

00:09:04.233 --> 00:09:05.930
and the US suffered.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:07.140
It just keeps going up.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.675
I mean, it hits record
high after record high.

00:09:09.675 --> 00:09:12.870
In February looking back,
it seems like hubris,

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.963
but investors often don't see
what's right in front of them.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:18.910
- Coronavirus was on the horizon,

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:22.140
but our investor day was
one of the most bolish

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.243
and exciting that we've ever had.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.980
- I remember talking to a
very, very prominent investor

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:29.698
Superbowl weekend down in Miami,

00:09:29.698 --> 00:09:32.261
where we had a discussion
with both of us said,

00:09:32.261 --> 00:09:35.677
"Boy, it seems like
there's a lot more risk

00:09:35.677 --> 00:09:37.907
"in this virus becoming a bigger issue

00:09:37.907 --> 00:09:39.307
"than the market's pricing."

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.213
- On on February 11th, we've
been asked to participate

00:09:43.213 --> 00:09:45.750
in the opening bell ceremony for NASDAQ

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.410
in celebration of our 20th anniversary.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:49.680
- Happy 20th to JBLU.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:52.960
- I had the pleasure of appearing

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.640
on Squawk Box in the morning
and was specifically asked

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:56.860
whether or not we were seeing

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.240
any impact from the coronavirus.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:00.440
- You want to start with coronavirus?

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:01.273
- Sure.

00:10:02.237 --> 00:10:03.840
- Is it really impacting things

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.070
in here in United States right now?

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:07.860
- For JetBlue, it's not impacting JetBlue

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.010
in any meaningful way.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:10.100
Boy was I wrong?

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:13.000
And there a headline that
I think will live with me

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.713
until I retire from this industry.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.500
- The Chinese government
basically shut the country down

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.013
and put the entire
populace under quarantine.

00:10:24.013 --> 00:10:26.513
(tense music)

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.965
Now, that had an immediate impact

00:10:33.965 --> 00:10:36.130
on Toyota's operations there.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:38.493
We couldn't make cars,
we couldn't sell cars.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.520
- We have over 700 screens now,

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:46.380
which is 1% of the screens in the country.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:49.560
And Chinese new year is
the biggest film going week

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:50.961
for us of the year in China.

00:10:50.961 --> 00:10:55.160
We typically do 10% of
our year in one week.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:58.390
And then we heard every
theater in China was closed.

00:10:58.390 --> 00:10:59.858
And then a day later, they said

00:10:59.858 --> 00:11:03.950
none of the big Chinese
movies would be released.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.894
What was the biggest part of our year

00:11:05.894 --> 00:11:09.310
was reduced to nothing overnight.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:12.860
- We produce about 70 billion
doses of medicine a year

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:14.940
in over 150 countries.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.350
We had to make the decision

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.260
to send all of our China operations home,

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:20.920
which is over 10,000 people.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:23.943
I think I began to wonder now,
this is something really big.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:27.270
- Movement around China,

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.980
especially around the Ubed
province was being restricted.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:33.350
So we had to let both our employees know,

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.370
and more broadly we communicated

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.690
that this could have a ripple effect

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:38.960
through other supply chains.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.500
And it wasn't clear at that time

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.053
how significant it would be.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.440
- We started having meetings
almost every other day

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:50.978
to really understand

00:11:50.978 --> 00:11:53.530
how do we pull together the right teams,

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:57.400
assessing the demand, the
supply chain challenges.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.240
We work together for
everything, from sourcing,

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.420
making sure that we had the
critical raw materials available

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.410
in order to be able to
manufacture respirators,

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:10.780
gearing up the equipment so
that we could run 24, seven

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:12.283
everywhere around the world.

00:12:13.296 --> 00:12:14.500
- By the end of February,

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.730
we had mobilized a crisis management team.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:21.640
I was meeting with my
CFO on a regular basis,

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:25.100
thinking very long and
hard about liquidity.

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:27.490
We were modeling every weekend,

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:31.780
looking at these numbers
that I think look horrific,

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:34.017
that looked like nothing I've ever seen

00:12:34.017 --> 00:12:36.000
only to be outdone

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.530
by the numbers that I
saw the next weekend,

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:39.770
which only got outdone

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:41.650
by the numbers I saw the following week,

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.623
as it went further and
further into the abyss.

00:12:46.868 --> 00:12:49.451
(mellow music)

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.023
- I remember the management team meeting

00:12:59.023 --> 00:13:02.480
that I had with my team on March 4th,

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.990
where we all sat in the same
conference room together.

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:08.730
Jamie came and spoke to us for an hour

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:10.457
and he said to us,

00:13:10.457 --> 00:13:12.127
"You should go read the history

00:13:12.127 --> 00:13:15.747
"of the Spanish flu pandemic in 1918."

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:22.970
It was a early brush fire in
the spring summer of that year.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.230
It kind of went away, and
then in the fall it came back

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:29.060
and it killed 50 million people.

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:32.090
And he said, this is going to be severe.

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:35.080
- 35 fatalities are linked
to the life care center

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.393
in Kirkland, that is the
hardest hit facility.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.060
- When I saw pictures of that nursing home

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:44.735
outside of Seattle,

00:13:44.735 --> 00:13:48.570
where families couldn't get
to see their family members

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:49.720
inside the nursing home,

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:53.763
because the virus had essentially
taken that facility over,

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:57.040
the heartbreaking pictures of people

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:00.200
with their noses pressed
up against the window

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.170
to try and communicate
with their loved ones,

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:03.483
that struck home with me.

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:13.610
- Airlines is where you
first started to see

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.200
the disruption from the virus.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.810
- As we stepped into
March, we started seeing

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:21.100
a fairly rapid decline
of customer bookings,

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:23.800
an increase in customer
no-show rates and just

00:14:24.733 --> 00:14:26.070
overall changing customer sentiment.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:28.190
We knew we were in
something very different

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:29.880
than what we'd experienced before.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.380
Very different than SARS,
very different than Zika

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.563
and quite frankly, very
different than nine 11.

00:14:38.718 --> 00:14:41.730
- Our occupancies so quickly
from the third week in February

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:44.340
to the first week in
March, almost fell in half

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:45.790
in some of our luxury brands.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:47.940
But it was really where
our group business was,

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.833
because of the international
visitation that was impacted.

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:54.360
- That time was a hectic time.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.770
Early in March,

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:59.290
government started putting
restrictions on large gatherings.

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:02.890
That's when we started in
that early March timeframe,

00:15:02.890 --> 00:15:04.270
really thinking about,

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:06.613
we may have to play games without fans.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:11.467
- I sat down with my
CFO and I said to him,

00:15:11.467 --> 00:15:14.547
"If the world shuts down, what
do our financials look like?

00:15:14.547 --> 00:15:15.937
"What's our burn rate?

00:15:15.937 --> 00:15:17.607
"How do we stop spending money?"

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:24.490
We called all our managers and
we said, "Cancel all costs."

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:27.537
We just said, stop it right now

00:15:27.537 --> 00:15:30.300
because we don't know
what the future entails.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:32.930
- We pulled all our
levers on liquidity early

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.970
because the reality is cash is king.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:37.390
I don't know how deep it's going.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:39.390
And so to protect the business,

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.420
I've got to make sure
that we have enough cash.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.840
We have a billion 750 credit facility.

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:44.673
We pulled it.

00:15:47.318 --> 00:15:50.068
(crowd cheering)

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.290
- I think this event for
us and the realization

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:58.020
of probably where we were going

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:01.423
hit the weekend of
March 8th, 9th and 10th.

00:16:03.769 --> 00:16:04.602
We were fortunate,

00:16:04.602 --> 00:16:08.040
we were hosting 15,000
people for a UFC fight.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.170
We had Bruno Mars at the park theater,

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.450
but the reality was over at Mirage,

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:14.760
we were hosting a women's
leadership conference.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:19.410
One of the guest speakers was
sick and contracted COVID-19.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.150
A guest speaker at an event of scale,

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:24.250
about eight or 900 ladies
was in VIP receptions,

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:26.670
went to several restaurants,
went to several shows,

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.880
met many people, shook a
lot of our employees' hands.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:32.310
It became a little daunting
into thinking about,

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:34.610
how are we going to
handle this going forward?

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.600
- When investors do finally
pull their head out of the sand,

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:47.860
they do it really quickly

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:50.560
and the market starts to tank fast.

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:53.290
So on March 9th, the
market barely gets open.

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:55.730
- [Woman] Trading has stopped.

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:57.020
Trading has stopped.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:58.730
- Stocks fall so fast that it triggers

00:16:58.730 --> 00:16:59.840
what we call a circuit breaker,

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:01.170
which is basically a time-out.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.840
It was put in place after the 1987 crash.

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:06.499
And it's basically an automatic halt.

00:17:06.499 --> 00:17:07.332
Everyone go back to your corner,

00:17:07.332 --> 00:17:08.190
think about what you've done.

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:10.840
Take a deep breath and we'll
try this again in 15 minutes.

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:13.260
And it hadn't been traded in 20 years.

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:16.050
It was a sign that people were panicking.

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:18.290
Investors were running for the exits

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:20.190
and they couldn't get out fast enough.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.470
March 11th for a lot of people

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:27.760
is the day that this became
real, very real, very quickly.

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:32.230
- We have the format the
assessment that COVID-19

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:35.850
can be characterized as a pandemic.

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:38.343
- To keep new cases from
entering our shores,

00:17:38.343 --> 00:17:41.160
we will be suspending all travel

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:45.400
from Europe to the United
States for the next 30 days.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:47.430
- On March 11th, we had a board meeting.

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:49.010
And the topic of that board meeting

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:51.500
was how we were gonna
deal with the coronavirus.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:53.340
There was an understanding that yes,

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:56.570
we might actually have to
play some games without fans.

00:17:56.570 --> 00:17:59.440
And at the time, it seemed unthinkable.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:01.146
And then of course that night

00:18:01.146 --> 00:18:05.610
is when we got our first confirmed
positive with Rudy Gobert

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.963
and we knew right then that
we had to suspend the season.

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.590
- The game tonight has been postponed.

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:17.590
You're all safe and take your time

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:19.480
in leaving the arena tonight

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.290
and do so in an orderly fashion.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:23.040
- What brought a lot
of that home for people

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.130
was that they watched
it happen in real time.

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:27.000
There's still games happening.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:28.535
- [Man] This is crazy.

00:18:28.535 --> 00:18:30.113
This can't be true.

00:18:30.113 --> 00:18:31.310
I mean it's not within the
realm of possibilities,

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:34.930
it's just the seem more like
out of a movie than reality.

00:18:34.930 --> 00:18:36.730
- And then very quickly
people are looking at back

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.100
at a press conference
that Rudy Gobert had given

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:40.420
where he makes a joke
out of the whole thing

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:41.986
and is touching all these microphones.

00:18:41.986 --> 00:18:45.623
It just starts to sink in
that this is not a joke.

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:48.530
- I remember thinking,

00:18:48.530 --> 00:18:50.870
wonder what they're
gonna talk about on ESPN.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:54.210
It's a sports network and
sports are shutting down.

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:57.570
And then thinking we sell food,

00:18:57.570 --> 00:19:00.170
whether everything from
whether it's ground beef

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:02.870
or sweeteners that go into the sodas

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:04.680
that are served for these stadiums.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:06.280
What's this gonna mean for our customers?

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:09.120
And you quickly go to start thinking about

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.152
second and third order consequences

00:19:11.152 --> 00:19:15.190
as stadiums and restaurants

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:18.760
and places of public
gathering start to close.

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:21.610
What does that mean for our
customers for their business?

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:23.690
What does it mean for our business

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:25.690
and what does it mean for our employees?

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:31.670
- And March 12th was the last
day I've been in the office.

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:33.880
March 12th also was the day

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:37.190
that the market took a severe downturn.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:39.000
And for our 5,000 advisors,

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:42.080
the average industry age of an
advisor are in their mid 50s

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:43.610
and they've seen many of these cycles

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:46.030
and many people remember 08 and 09.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.420
But we also have a number of
advisors who were in their 30s

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:51.700
and they don't remember 08 and 09

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:54.370
and that likely was the
worst day of their career

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.510
and they hadn't seen volatility like that.

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:57.930
We also have a number of clients

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.420
who had probably never
seen volatility like that.

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:01.853
First-time investors.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:08.520
- I was surprised at how
hard IMAX's stock got hit

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.190
'cause we were incredibly crushed.

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:15.630
Our stock had been trading
generally in the $20 range

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:18.770
and it went down to six or seven dollars

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:20.320
and within a matter of days.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:23.010
When I was allowed, I
actually went into the market

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:24.420
and I bought some stock,

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:26.710
but I thought I would
also be sending a message

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:30.150
to our shareholders that
I believed in the company

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.023
and I believe we could survive this.

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:35.320
- March 12th stands out

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:36.840
and so far as I was on a flight to Boston

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:39.470
visiting with our crew
members on a pre-planned trip.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:42.940
The questions being asked
changed almost overnight.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:45.110
The questions our crew
members started asking

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:48.810
were not just about my health,
but am I going to have a job?

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:50.430
What's this going to do to JetBlue?

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:52.390
Can we survive this?

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:55.660
It all came to a crescendo
at that point in time

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:58.750
when you realize that this
is a much bigger thing

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:00.253
than anybody expected.

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:05.710
- We saw many of our advised
clients move and de-risk

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:09.820
and get into cash or short
term cash like strategies,

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:12.537
not surprisingly, particularly our clients

00:21:12.537 --> 00:21:14.990
who are nearing retirement
just wanted to play it safe.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:17.300
The advisors were still
going into the branches

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:19.510
because we were trying to
stay open for our clients

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:20.530
as long as possible.

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:22.130
Markets were super volatile.

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:24.420
People wanted to meet with their advisor.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:26.790
- New York state on pause.

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:30.520
- A statewide order for
people to stay at home.

00:21:30.520 --> 00:21:33.220
- We must enact an
immediate stay-at-home order

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:36.323
for the sake of Illinois.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.980
- So there's a whole group of companies

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:46.890
whose business models
just don't make sense

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:48.930
when there's a pandemic going on.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:51.150
Bars, restaurants, movie, theaters,

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:52.920
sports stadiums, casinos,

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.710
anything that involves
having lots of people

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.560
in close quarters just doesn't work.

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:59.390
So those shut down basically overnight.

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:01.372
- I made a lot of phone calls and I said,

00:22:01.372 --> 00:22:03.180
we should be leaders in this.

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:04.350
Let's not wait.

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:07.140
We should be upfront that
it's the right message

00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:08.760
that we're really concerned

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:11.020
about the health and
safety of our customers.

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:13.320
To be fair to the people I was talking to,

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:16.308
blockbusters were scheduled to open soon.

00:22:16.308 --> 00:22:19.860
They had pre-sold tickets to theaters

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:22.771
and this lunatic is saying shut down.

00:22:22.771 --> 00:22:26.560
- What happened in the early
into middle part of March

00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:29.530
was we were constantly adjusting

00:22:29.530 --> 00:22:31.860
to see if we could maintain production,

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:34.920
but there was a point in
time in that timeframe

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:38.300
where a number of
jurisdictions basically said,

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:39.730
we're shutting down.

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:43.470
Our pipelines by design are pretty thin.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:45.030
We don't carry a lot of inventory.

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:46.440
That's the Toyota way.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:47.860
Inventory is waste.

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:51.030
When our production
stopped, it had the impact

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:54.233
of almost drying up the typical

00:22:54.233 --> 00:22:58.730
90 to 120 days worth of
inventory that we might have

00:22:58.730 --> 00:23:01.280
for certainly for some of
our more popular models.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:03.890
Now it's much less than that

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:06.220
in ways where we are living hand to mouth,

00:23:06.220 --> 00:23:08.870
to make sure that we satisfy
the demand of customers.

00:23:10.931 --> 00:23:12.230
We got one more bad news as you know,

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:14.160
the market went to heck.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:15.610
Our occupancies were going down.

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:19.010
At one point, our forecast
for this building Bellagio

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:20.360
was like single digit.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:23.110
It became clear to us that
we were gonna have to close.

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:26.760
In this state, we are
the largest employer,

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.230
largest payer of taxes.

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:31.430
We are this community's economy.

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:34.690
We had made a decision on
the 14th to close everything.

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:37.480
We notified the governor
on Sunday afternoon.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.270
And by Sunday at six o'clock that night,

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:41.660
he had gone here in
Nevada on the television

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:43.120
and mandated a closure.

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:45.210
- The most effective course of action

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:48.080
is to direct all Nevadans to stay home

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:51.917
and for non essential businesses
to close to the public

00:23:51.917 --> 00:23:53.403
for 30 days.

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:57.740
- We find ourselves in a closed mode.

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:02.460
For us, that means about 270
million a month in expenses.

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:03.413
It's our burn rate.

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:06.830
- There was no choice.

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:08.410
There was no business.

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:10.764
There were no revenues,
there was no demand.

00:24:10.764 --> 00:24:15.000
Even if you'd been able
to reduce your workforce,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.340
you still have to pay your debt service,

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:19.390
you still got to pay utilities and taxes

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:21.350
and all of these costs.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:24.700
No business is built for zero revenues.

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:25.863
It's just not done.

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:30.281
- The Dow has its worst
single day of history.

00:24:30.281 --> 00:24:31.930
I mean, just hundreds and
hundreds of billions of dollars

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:34.491
of stock market value is just evaporating.

00:24:34.491 --> 00:24:37.670
- We were watching changes in markets.

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:40.693
We were watching companies
borrow more aggressively.

00:24:40.693 --> 00:24:43.620
And one of the things that
we started talking about

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:45.590
was there always been a stigma

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:47.910
associated with borrowing
from the FED window.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.453
So collectively the CEOs
of the eight large banks

00:24:50.453 --> 00:24:52.530
got on a call together and we said,

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:55.736
does it make sense for
us to all just borrow

00:24:55.736 --> 00:24:59.020
a nominal amount to show
that we're all in unison,

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:00.840
that this is not something
that should have stigma.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:03.677
And if it's necessary to
keep the banking system

00:25:03.677 --> 00:25:06.120
functioning smoothly for our clients,

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.085
we're all prepared to do it.

00:25:08.085 --> 00:25:09.880
- [Bill] And Hilton is the
Canary in the coal mine.

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:11.600
This is an incredibly well capitalized,

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:13.990
amazing dominant global company

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:15.830
that actually doesn't own many hotels.

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:17.540
It just collects royalties.

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:19.660
Down from like 120 to 50.

00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:21.880
Okay, it's going to zero

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:24.965
along with every other hotel company.

00:25:24.965 --> 00:25:26.470
- Bill Ackman is a well-known
hedge fund investor.

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:27.687
He buys vixen companies,

00:25:27.687 --> 00:25:30.610
and is pretty vocal about
his opinions on them.

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:32.200
He's positioned in Hilton.

00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:34.950
And he went on CNBC and
said, among other things

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.010
that stock might go to zero.

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:39.190
- You say Hilton is going to zero, I mean.

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:42.510
- [Bill] 'Cause no business can survive

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:45.703
a period of 18 months without revenue.

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:47.950
- And as he's talking,

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.980
the market is just falling
and falling and falling

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:52.090
and it eventually triggers
another circuit breaker.

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:55.710
- I think he was trying to
make a point, which was,

00:25:55.710 --> 00:26:00.710
people should be taking this
COVID-19 thing very seriously

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:03.530
because it could have
a devastating impact.

00:26:03.530 --> 00:26:04.980
And reality is, he was right.

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:09.050
- We saw our revenue go

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:12.560
from nearly $20 million a day to zero

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:15.202
and even negative almost overnight.

00:26:15.202 --> 00:26:16.780
A number of states

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:19.620
were putting into place
travel quarantines.

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:22.610
You had governors and others talking about

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:24.500
flying only for a central travel.

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:27.810
In March, our cash burn was
about $15 million a day.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:29.270
When you start doing the math,

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:32.200
you realize very quickly
that there's an end point

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:34.380
where you just frankly, run out of money.

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:36.620
And the questions that we had to address

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:39.910
for our crew members were what
are we doing to cut costs?

00:26:39.910 --> 00:26:42.049
Standing down projects, freezing hiring,

00:26:42.049 --> 00:26:44.210
I'm looking at any lever we could pull

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:45.923
to try to reduce our cash burn.

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:49.060
It became very apparent

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:51.310
that the amount of
flying that we were doing

00:26:51.310 --> 00:26:53.430
was going to have to be
reduced significantly.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:56.110
And the operational
complexity of doing that,

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:58.870
it's not just limited to
changing people's shifts.

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:01.506
We have this fleet of over 260 aircraft.

00:27:01.506 --> 00:27:03.997
And where do you park all those planes?

00:27:03.997 --> 00:27:07.283
We needed to park them out in the desert.

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:15.730
- [Reporter] It was a record breaking fall

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:17.619
for US retail sales in March.

00:27:17.619 --> 00:27:20.930
- And then there are companies
that are forced to shut down,

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:22.600
but like they don't have
any customers anymore.

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:24.580
There's just a total collapse in demand.

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:26.480
People stopped shopping.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:28.490
They just stopped going to stores.

00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:30.760
- [Reporter] This is the
biggest decline in retail sales

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:34.173
since the government started
keeping track at 1992.

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:37.440
- There were a lot of companies

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:39.713
that don't have the
option of shutting down.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:43.510
They make things that people need.

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:47.260
- We are being asked by global
governments to stay open,

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:50.503
to make sure that food
could get to the consumers.

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:54.740
The food supply chain is complicated.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:57.424
There's a lot of steps in the process

00:27:57.424 --> 00:28:01.220
from the cattle ranchers
and poultry farmers

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:04.390
and the feed producers that
make the feed for the livestock

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:07.560
and the farmers that tend to the livestock

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:09.551
and then to the production facilities,

00:28:09.551 --> 00:28:12.550
and then ultimately to
the transportation system

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:14.183
that bring them to the stores.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:17.460
- [Reporter] The number
of coronavirus cases

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:20.460
in the United States is
growing exponentially.

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:22.860
- [Reporter] Store shelves
nationwide are dwindling

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:24.410
or totally empty.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:28.040
- People start panic buying
anything and everything.

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:33.860
It starts to feel a
little bit like in times.

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:35.540
- Pre-loading their panic buying

00:28:35.540 --> 00:28:37.580
increased consumption
beyond what we could make

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:41.513
in any given week and so
inventories were drawn down.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:45.860
That did put a lot of
pressure into the system

00:28:45.860 --> 00:28:48.100
for us to maximize production.

00:28:48.100 --> 00:28:50.830
What we wanted to do is
get the assets that we had

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:54.410
running 24 hours a day, seven days a week

00:28:54.410 --> 00:28:57.170
and they increased production
by several million cases

00:28:57.170 --> 00:28:59.150
across our total system.

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:01.130
- These companies have to stay open.

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:04.450
Their first priority is
keeping their employees safe

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:06.493
and keeping the business operating.

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:11.900
- We moved quickly early on to
get as much PPE as we could.

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:14.777
Personal protective equipment
to take temperatures

00:29:14.777 --> 00:29:19.777
amongst people going in and
if anyone showed any symptoms,

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:21.860
they could not go back in.

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:26.200
We added plastic shields in
between the workers on the line.

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:28.630
In the back of your mind,
you hold that optimism.

00:29:28.630 --> 00:29:32.040
Maybe we'll get through this
with nobody getting impacted,

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:33.690
nobody getting infected.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:37.340
But when you have 160,000
employees that are out and about

00:29:37.340 --> 00:29:40.470
not only in our facilities,
but in their own communities,

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:42.570
you realize the odds of that are very low.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:46.920
- And then there are the companies

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:49.573
that we're all turning to, to fix this.

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:53.980
You've got companies making
masks and respirators.

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:55.670
You've got pharmaceutical companies

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:57.670
who are getting to work on treatments.

00:29:57.670 --> 00:29:59.270
So these are companies that
not only do they not have

00:29:59.270 --> 00:30:00.410
the option of shutting down,

00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:02.750
the entire world is
looking at them and saying,

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:05.540
this is not just a
business question for you.

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:08.440
This is an existential thing
that we need you to do.

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:10.760
You have to do a better
job than you used to do

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:13.260
and do it under unbelievably
trying circumstances.

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:17.880
- We have a few late stage
phase three clinical trials,

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:19.050
of repurposing medicines.

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:22.160
And then we have a very
large drug discovery effort

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:24.700
trying to see, can we find what we call

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:27.190
a pan-coronavirus drug that requires

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:28.820
new scientific discoveries,

00:30:28.820 --> 00:30:30.840
new scale up new clinical trials.

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:32.740
And that is a much bigger challenge

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:35.760
because now you're asking
clinical trial sites

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:38.850
that are now focused on trying
to respond to a pandemic

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:40.784
to get a trial up and running.

00:30:40.784 --> 00:30:43.940
Can you still enroll
patients into the study

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:45.870
if healthcare systems are closing down?

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:49.350
- A number of cases soaring just today.

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:52.320
More than 24,000 now nationwide

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:54.130
New Jersey today becoming the sixth state

00:30:54.130 --> 00:30:56.700
to close all nonessential businesses.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:58.000
- [Reporter] The president invoked

00:30:58.000 --> 00:30:59.570
the Defense Production Act

00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:02.766
to prioritize production of
items under government contract

00:31:02.766 --> 00:31:06.940
and to allocate scarce items
where they are needed most.

00:31:06.940 --> 00:31:09.520
- The order authorizes the head of FEMA

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:14.420
to acquire as many N 95
masks from the company, 3M

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:15.570
as is deemed necessary.

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:20.620
- We had significantly
increased the production rate

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:23.000
for respirators here in the US,

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.950
we'd gone from maybe 22 million in January

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:29.000
to more than 35 million in March.

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:31.730
We could see that this
was really coming at us

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:33.560
pretty fast here in the US.

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:37.270
So not only are we looking at
how do we continue to drive up

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:39.690
manufacturing of our own products,

00:31:39.690 --> 00:31:44.150
but also what else can we do
from other parts of the world?

00:31:44.150 --> 00:31:45.650
- So it doesn't take long

00:31:45.650 --> 00:31:48.319
before you start hearing talk of bailouts.

00:31:48.319 --> 00:31:52.610
- The airline industry alone,
seeking a $50 billion bailout,

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:54.640
but should these companies get relief

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:55.933
with no strings attached.

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:02.150
- The industry as a whole came
together in a remarkable way.

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:05.750
CEOs from every airline
were down in Washington,

00:32:05.750 --> 00:32:08.840
trying to make sure that all
the decision makers understood

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:10.790
how vital and critical
the airline industry is

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:11.793
to this economy.

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:14.520
- We were number one,

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:17.210
really reinforcing with the administration

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:20.230
that the employment
situation was gonna be dire,

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:22.040
that our industry was gonna lose

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:23.560
millions and millions of jobs,

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:25.570
and we needed the federal government

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:27.763
to help take care of those teams.

00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:38.327
- The employees are the
ones most hit by this.

00:32:38.327 --> 00:32:43.160
When you lay off 62,000
employees, it's meaningful.

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:45.200
But I will give the
federal government credit.

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:48.150
The Cares Act came
together fairly quickly.

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:49.243
- Thank you all very much.

00:32:49.243 --> 00:32:50.780
It's a very important day.

00:32:50.780 --> 00:32:54.063
I've signed the single biggest
economic relief package

00:32:54.063 --> 00:32:56.350
in American history and I must say,

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:58.220
or any other package by the way.

00:32:58.220 --> 00:33:00.612
- So on March 27th, President Trump signs,

00:33:00.612 --> 00:33:04.140
the $2 trillion coronavirus aid package,

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:06.061
the Cares Act into law.

00:33:06.061 --> 00:33:07.230
This dwarfs any kind of stimulus

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:10.040
that was handed out during
the 2008 financial crisis.

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:12.772
So it includes billions
of dollars for airlines,

00:33:12.772 --> 00:33:16.247
for hospitals, for state
and local governments.

00:33:16.247 --> 00:33:19.370
It has a large, small
business lending program

00:33:19.370 --> 00:33:21.320
that's meant to sort of keep paying people

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:23.570
who would otherwise have been laid off.

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:25.440
This is a massive amount
of government spending,

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:28.893
and it's meant to put a band-aid
over this broken economy.

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:32.360
Part of what we've had to
take a very hard look at

00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:35.130
is how we make the payroll
dollars under the Cares Act,

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:39.550
work well for us and really
be clear with our crew members

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:42.860
about what we expect the future to hold.

00:33:42.860 --> 00:33:44.440
- We have not ever advocated

00:33:44.440 --> 00:33:48.400
for any sort of help from
the government for Hilton.

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:50.410
And so what we were advocating there

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:51.243
on behalf of our owners,

00:33:51.243 --> 00:33:53.791
many of whom are small medium-sized,

00:33:53.791 --> 00:33:57.200
entrepreneurially based businesses was,

00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:59.850
we needed to help create
a bridge of liquidity

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:01.820
to help them get to the other side.

00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:03.720
- One of the things that's so interesting

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:05.719
about the monetary and fiscal policy

00:34:05.719 --> 00:34:07.644
is it's really created liquidity

00:34:07.644 --> 00:34:10.240
and support for large businesses.

00:34:10.240 --> 00:34:11.710
It's allowed large businesses,

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:13.420
even those that are in distress

00:34:13.420 --> 00:34:15.763
to finance forward, very, very actively.

00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:20.810
But small businesses and
especially very small businesses

00:34:20.810 --> 00:34:23.053
are just devastated by this crisis.

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:29.240
- And the worst part is by late March,

00:34:29.240 --> 00:34:30.840
it's clear that this isn't over

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:33.970
and that it's time to
start packing sandbags.

00:34:33.970 --> 00:34:38.510
- We had around $380 million in cash,

00:34:38.510 --> 00:34:41.367
and we looked at our burn
rate and we concluded

00:34:41.367 --> 00:34:44.863
that without massive layoffs
and things like that,

00:34:44.863 --> 00:34:48.440
we probably spend about
$10 million a month.

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:50.120
So we looked at it and we said,

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:52.330
we have about two and a half years

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:54.320
and a zero revenue environment.

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:57.340
And that was a really
important element for us,

00:34:57.340 --> 00:35:02.240
because at that time, the
street and constituencies

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:05.760
were really focused on how
long companies could survive

00:35:05.760 --> 00:35:07.410
and would they be going out of business?

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:10.820
And I think that was a really
important message for us,

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:12.853
that we had the money to survive.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:16.320
- We were fortunate last year,

00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:19.440
we sold off about $8 billion
worth of real estate.

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:22.040
With that $8 billion,
we sold down some debt,

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:26.330
but between what we left and
a bond offering we just did

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:30.030
for 750 million, we're
sitting on about 4.6 billion

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:31.930
in capital and liquidity.

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:34.400
So we have money to sustain us

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:36.893
and it will guide what
we do going forward.

00:35:40.820 --> 00:35:43.890
- The thing that's really
different about this pandemic

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:48.560
is that it is global and that
the demand has been so high.

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:52.610
Quadrupling of capacity or output from 3M

00:35:52.610 --> 00:35:54.720
is still not sufficient.

00:35:54.720 --> 00:35:57.300
The increase in manufacturing

00:35:57.300 --> 00:35:59.210
from even some of our competitors

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:01.780
is still not sufficient
to meet the demand.

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:05.380
- A lot of focus came on
the meat production industry

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:10.010
because so many facilities
had been shutting down.

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:14.470
- Union officials now say
164 of the nearly 900 workers

00:36:14.470 --> 00:36:16.600
at the Cargill meat packing plant

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:18.790
have tested positive for the coronavirus.

00:36:18.790 --> 00:36:22.540
- The facility we closed in Pennsylvania

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:26.660
came back on after 17 days
and is back running again.

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:29.340
So I think that's the best
thing we can do as an industry

00:36:29.340 --> 00:36:32.983
is get adequate testing,
continue to have adequate PPE,

00:36:34.070 --> 00:36:37.820
but get the plants up and
running as quickly as possible

00:36:37.820 --> 00:36:40.383
so that store shelves can stay stocked.

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:52.153
- There's really no way in a
pandemic to keep everyone safe

00:36:52.153 --> 00:36:53.840
if people have to be gathering.

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:55.820
If they have to be working in a factory

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:58.593
or working in a retail
store, there's always risk.

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:05.330
- What kept us up at
night was figuring out

00:37:05.330 --> 00:37:07.780
if there was in fact,
a safe and healthy way

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:08.853
to return to play.

00:37:09.790 --> 00:37:14.790
There was no guarantee that
you can do it in 100% safe way.

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.693
And so this is literally
matters of life and death.

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:22.360
And so that weighs on you

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:25.523
because you don't want
to put anybody at risk.

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:30.703
That is what makes the weight
of these decisions so heavy.

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:33.420
- We lost an Empire casino,

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:36.942
one of our places back in New
York, we lost the horseman.

00:37:36.942 --> 00:37:40.400
And so it was actually the
first death within our family.

00:37:40.400 --> 00:37:43.570
It just took on a different
context from that point

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:45.830
and you began to realize
the responsibility

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:47.910
of you're not only
accountable to a business,

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:50.913
but at this point, you're
accountable for lives.

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:55.300
- And we lost our first crew member,

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:58.410
that frightened so many crew members,

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:01.840
but also brought home the
reality of this pandemic

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:03.953
and that it wasn't going away.

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:07.990
- One of the stories
that that really hit me

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:11.260
was the first death we had at
one of our production sites.

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:14.780
I think this was a moment
where it became very real

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:18.440
that we have this
difficult balance to walk.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:19.960
We make these medicines

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:23.060
that hundreds of millions
of people depend on

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:26.920
and yet at the same time, we
want to protect the people

00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:29.520
who come bravely into the office,

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:32.440
into the manufacturing sites
or our clinical trial sites,

00:38:32.440 --> 00:38:35.134
where the healthcare
workers were working bravely

00:38:35.134 --> 00:38:38.233
to keep the hospitals running.

00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:42.662
- There's no magic bullet.

00:38:42.662 --> 00:38:45.950
There's no magic vaccine or therapy.

00:38:45.950 --> 00:38:47.350
It's just behaviors.

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:49.709
- On March 31st, last day of the month,

00:38:49.709 --> 00:38:52.440
the US overtakes China in
the official death count.

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:55.860
Total number of confirmed cases
at that point are 160,000,

00:38:55.860 --> 00:38:58.550
which is the most of any
country in the world.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:00.010
American business comes into March

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:02.830
riding an 11-year economic expansion.

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:06.000
By the end of the month,
the economy is in tatters,

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:08.099
10 million people had lost their jobs.

00:39:08.099 --> 00:39:11.530
- Unemployment numbers are
at unprecedented levels.

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:12.640
- [Reporter] The drive-through,

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:15.357
a symbol of America's capitalist invention

00:39:15.357 --> 00:39:19.583
now being used to process
its millions of unemployment.

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:25.050
- Literally, about a
third of American workers

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:29.080
were vulnerable, vulnerable
either to being laid off,

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:34.020
vulnerable to furloughs,
vulnerable to reduced work hours.

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:36.320
Now that's clearly a much larger number

00:39:36.320 --> 00:39:38.696
than actual unemployment.

00:39:38.696 --> 00:39:42.557
- The 500 largest companies
in the US are worth

00:39:42.557 --> 00:39:46.233
$3 trillion less than they
were before the month began.

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:49.680
The market, which has just
been at all time highs

00:39:49.680 --> 00:39:54.290
in February is officially
in a bear market territory,

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:55.843
20% off from the peak.

00:40:00.860 --> 00:40:04.770
- The pandemic closed
the book on one chapter

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:06.030
for American business.

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:07.450
The decade that proceeded it

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:10.210
was one of the most successful in history

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:12.200
and it ended quickly.

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:15.593
That's a lot of whiplash
for business to absorb.

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:19.590
- Given our access to information

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:21.760
because we have such a
big footprint in Asia

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:25.860
why did all of us and by
the way, not just all of us,

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:29.130
but so many feel that the chance of this

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:31.800
spreading into a global pandemic was low?

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:33.631
We all tend to look at things

00:40:33.631 --> 00:40:37.411
through a lens of the
experiences that we've had.

00:40:37.411 --> 00:40:40.800
- The difference is we are
much more globally connected

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:43.660
from air travel, the way
supply chains work now

00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:45.560
than perhaps 10 or 12 years ago

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:49.110
when SARS and MERS were
really taking place.

00:40:49.110 --> 00:40:50.860
We've seen the market hit record high

00:40:50.860 --> 00:40:52.450
after record highs since March,

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:54.360
but there's a lot of rebuilding to do.

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:55.780
And companies are now grappling

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:58.480
with what kind of foundation
they're rebuilding on.

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:00.840
- What might prove to be
features of the economy

00:41:00.840 --> 00:41:01.673
coming out of this,

00:41:01.673 --> 00:41:03.580
one is the idea of what do we think of

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:04.840
as essential infrastructure,

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:06.790
essential capabilities for our company?

00:41:06.790 --> 00:41:08.869
We've now all rediscovered that in fact,

00:41:08.869 --> 00:41:12.130
the probably the most
essential infrastructure

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:14.720
in this moment, as I said,
digital infrastructures.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:17.060
I don't think you're
going to see investments

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:19.450
in digitization slow down, actually.

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:21.829
I think they may continue to accelerate.

00:41:21.829 --> 00:41:24.048
- One of the things that
we're thinking about

00:41:24.048 --> 00:41:27.750
is how do you continue to engage fans?

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:31.890
How do you continue to engage
people who love your sport,

00:41:31.890 --> 00:41:34.060
who can't actually there physically?

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:37.800
And this is why technology, I think

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:39.934
and the integration of that technology,

00:41:39.934 --> 00:41:42.007
those are the kinds of innovations

00:41:42.007 --> 00:41:44.133
that have come out of this pandemic.

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:47.390
- I think one of the capabilities

00:41:47.390 --> 00:41:48.660
that I think companies are going to now

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:51.260
have to think hard about and build for,

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:52.950
which is how do we build resilience,

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:56.066
even if it comes at the
expense of some efficiency.

00:41:56.066 --> 00:41:57.970
- The Toyota way of production,

00:41:57.970 --> 00:42:01.270
including just in time supply of parts

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:05.410
or more rigorous focus on
very efficient manufacturing,

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:07.040
that's not going to change.

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:10.470
What may change within that is
how we support our partners,

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:12.840
our suppliers, to be resilient

00:42:12.840 --> 00:42:17.083
in order to provide just in
time parts and production.

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:22.970
- It takes in a place like
this, in Bellagio about 70%

00:42:22.970 --> 00:42:24.633
normally just to break even.

00:42:24.633 --> 00:42:26.953
Even these are large ships, if you will.

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:30.610
And so to the extent
we're not in that mode,

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:32.373
we're probably not making money.

00:42:34.690 --> 00:42:35.790
Our principle is going to be,

00:42:35.790 --> 00:42:38.370
it's going to be some
time before we get there.

00:42:38.370 --> 00:42:40.710
And so how can we look at this operation?

00:42:40.710 --> 00:42:43.570
How can we look at this
business and regear it

00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:45.830
so that we can do that
much more effectively

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:47.383
at a much lower occupancy rate?

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:52.000
- I think there's been some
other different kinds of changes

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:54.290
at the level of society and the economy

00:42:54.290 --> 00:42:56.260
is becoming even more apparent

00:42:56.260 --> 00:42:58.720
about the often the
disconnect that happens

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:01.740
between successful corporations

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:03.940
and successful parts of the economy

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:06.550
and the parts of society
that get left behind.

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:08.964
Those might've been abstract
kind of conversations

00:43:08.964 --> 00:43:11.680
in a kind of theoretical abstract way,

00:43:11.680 --> 00:43:13.560
but I think most business leaders

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:16.730
have come into personal
direct experience with that

00:43:16.730 --> 00:43:17.820
in this moment.

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:19.392
- This won't last forever

00:43:19.392 --> 00:43:22.770
and some companies will be better for it.

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:25.530
Some will be stronger for
having sort of planned

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:27.540
and been flexible and gotten through it

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:29.520
and some will just never come back.

00:43:29.520 --> 00:43:31.360
- As an investments business,

00:43:31.360 --> 00:43:34.770
we teach our clients to
look at the long-term goal,

00:43:34.770 --> 00:43:36.780
and I think that's how you run a business.

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:38.090
You run a business for the long-term.

00:43:38.090 --> 00:43:39.623
You're prepared for the worst.

00:43:40.770 --> 00:43:42.133
This was the worst.

00:43:51.146 --> 00:43:53.646
(tense music)

